+# How a Genkit flow streams output
+
+> Note: the Genkit MCP server and `genkit --non-interactive docs:*` CLI commands
+> both failed to connect/fetch in this environment, so this was derived from
+> reading the type declarations shipped in `node_modules/genkit` and
+> `node_modules/@genkit-ai/core` (`flow.d.ts`, `action-DlkZMCP7.d.ts`,
+> `client/client.d.ts`) rather than the hosted docs.
+
+## Defining side
+
+Give the flow a `streamSchema` and accept a second argument — an
+`ActionFnArg`/`FlowSideChannel` — whose `sendChunk` function pushes one chunk
+at a time to the caller. The flow still returns/resolves its normal
+`outputSchema` value at the end.
+
+```ts
+export const myStreamingFlow = ai.defineFlow(
+  {
+    name: 'myStreamingFlow',
+    inputSchema: z.string(),
+    outputSchema: z.string(),
+    streamSchema: z.string(), // schema of each streamed chunk
+  },
+  async (input, { sendChunk }) => {
+    const { stream, response } = ai.generateStream({
+      prompt: input,
+    });
+
+    let full = '';
+    for await (const chunk of stream) {
+      full += chunk.text;
+      sendChunk(chunk.text); // push a chunk to the caller
+    }
+
+    await response;
+    return full; // final output, resolved once streaming is done
+  }
+);
+```
+
+## Calling side (in-process, e.g. from another flow or server code)
+
+Call `.stream()` on the flow/action instead of invoking it directly. It
+returns `{ stream, output }`: `stream` is an `AsyncGenerator` of chunks
+(matching `streamSchema`), and `output` is a `Promise` that resolves to the
+final return value (matching `outputSchema`).
+
+```ts
+const { stream, output } = myStreamingFlow.stream('tell me a story');
+
+for await (const chunk of stream) {
+  console.log('chunk:', chunk);
+}
+
+console.log('final output:', await output);
+```
+
+## Calling side (remote, over HTTP — deployed flow)
+
+Use `streamFlow` from `genkit/beta/client`. Same shape: `stream` to iterate
+chunks, `output` to await the final result.
+
+```ts
+import { streamFlow } from 'genkit/beta/client';
+
+const response = streamFlow({
+  url: 'https://my-flow-deployed-url',
+  input: 'foo',
+});
+
+for await (const chunk of response.stream) {
+  console.log(chunk);
+}
+console.log(await response.output);
+```
+
+(There is also a non-streaming `runFlow` with the same `url`/`input` shape
+for plain request/response calls.)
